Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

_3D.Poses.getMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
Returns the position, which the MU would take on the specified PE (production element).
Remarks
This is the same position which would be approached with the method
_3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition.
Syntax
<Path>(MU:object, TargetPE:any[, RelPos/AbsPos:real/length]) -> real[3]
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter MU of data type object designates the MU instance to be measured when moving to the
target place.
• The parameter TargetPE of data type any designates the place to which the item is to move. This can be
a specific PE, or an object if the object only has one PE.
• The optional parameter RelPos/AbsPos designates a value of data type real or length.
If the value has the data type real, it designates the relative position on the object. The relative position
can be a value between 0 and 1. 0 designates the start of the animation and 1 the end of the animation,
i.e., the value 100 %.
If the value has the data type length, it designates the absolute position on the object as the distance
starting at the beginning of the object.
This parameter will only be used if TargetPE specifies a length-oriented place, e.g. a Conveyor or a
Transporter with a length-oriented load bay.
If you do not specify the parameter, it takes the value 0.0.
Return Value
The return value is an array with three values of data type real.
It designates the X-position, the Y-position, and the Z-position of the MU on the PE.
Examples
var dropPos = Robot._3D.Poses.getMUAnimationPosition(@, 
ParallelStation[1,2])
_3D.Poses.getMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
12-928 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

