_3D.Poses.moveTo [SimTalk]
Moves to one or several poses of the object designated by <Path>.
Remarks
Whenever you change the Lower Limit/upper Limit, the Velocity, the Acceleration, or the Joint Type of
an animatable object, the initial position for the next pose run that affects this object returns to 0.
When all pose runs of an object reached their destination, Plant Simulation enters an EndOfTime event
into the Event List.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Path>._3D.Poses.moveTo(Pose:string[, TimeSpan:time]) → time
<Path>._3D.Poses.moveTo(Poses:array[string][, TimeSpan:time]]) → time
<Path>._3D.Poses.moveTo(Poses:array[string][, TimeSpans:array[time]]) → 
time
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Pose of data type string sets the name of the pose toward which the object moves.
The parameter Poses designates an array of data type string containing the names of several
consecutive poses. The object moves to the designated poses sequentially one after the other.
If you specify invalid parameters or if you specify a pose that contains an animatable object that is
part of a movement that is still active, the method opens the Method Debugger with an error. In this
context a pose movement always consists of all poses that are specified together with the method
_3D.Poses.moveTo. It stays active until the last pose is reached.
If the object is to move to a pose named "init" and no pose with that name exists, the object moves
to an implicit pose which sets all animatable objects of the object designated by <Path> to 0.
• The optional parameter TimeSpan of data type time sets the duration of the movement.
The optional parameter TimeSpans designates an array of data type time, which sets the durations of
the movement.
If you do not specify the parameter, the pose movement is run with the time which results from the
speeds from the joint definitions of the involved animatable objects.
If you specify the parameter, it means the following:
_3D.Poses.moveTo [SimTalk]
12-932 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

◦ If you specify a single time, the entire pose movement has to take place within the specified time.
The time relations of the joints and the individual pose movements among each other are retained.
If the instruction Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1", "Pose2"]) takes 3 seconds, of
which 1 second is allotted to Pose1 and 2 seconds are allotted to Pose2, then the instruction
Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1", "Pose2"], 7.5) takes 7.5 seconds of which 2.5
seconds are allotted to Pose1 and 5 seconds are allotted to Pose2.
◦ If you specify a sequence of times in an array, the first pose is to be reached within the first time
span, the second pose in the second time span, etc. You have to specify as many time spans as you
defined poses.
The instruction Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1", "Pose2"], 7.5) from the example
above and the instruction Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1", "Pose2"], [2.5, 5])
thus are equivalent. You can only specify an array of times if you also specified an array of poses.
The instruction Station._3D.Poses.moveTo("Pose1", [7.5]) results in an error although
the same number of poses is specified as the number of time spans. The instruction
Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1"], [7.5]) does not result in an error, but is
unnecessarily complicated because the instruction Station._3D.Poses.moveTo("Pose1",
7.5)) yields the same result without specifying an array at all.
Return Value
The return value has the data type time.
It designates the time span of the simulation time which the designated pose movement will probably
take, provided it will not be interrupted.
Example
Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1", "Pose2"])
Station._3D.Poses.moveTo(["Pose1", "Pose2"], 7.5)
Station._3D.Poses.moveTo("Pose1", 7.5)
