_3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate [SimTalk]
Moves the object designated by <Path> to the specified coordinate with its first animation point to the
designated coordinate and to the designated rotation as long as the identified robot configuration supports
this. This works without you having to define a pose.
Remarks
_3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate does not support the four axis robot with ball joint gripper. The object
corresponds to one of the supported robot configurations.
Plant Simulation checks the attribute _3D.AnimationObject to identify the robot configuration. The
objects, which are located between the simulation object, which is excluded, and _3D.AnimationObject,
which is included, are checked for the appropriate robot configuration with which the pose movement is
executed.
Plant Simulation checks if a part of the axes configuration matches a supported robot type from the
simulation object level to the gripper object. A robot type with more axes takes precedence over a robot
type with fewer axes.
Note
If Plant Simulation cannot identify the robot configuration, this is considered an error and the Method
Debugger will open.
Note
Not reaching the destination coordinate/rotation is not considered an error and does not open the
Method Debugger. An unsuitable, but recognizable axes configuration will not be recognized, if eight
axes exist, for example, but only the last three axes are recognized as part of a simpler robot type.
Note
Plant Simulation does not use joint limits in this method. This means, that you can move to a position
beyond the limits you specified.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Path>._3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate(Coordinate:length[3], Rotation:real/
real[4][, TimeSpan:time]) -> time
_3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 12-935
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Coordinate is an array of data type length with three values which designates the
destination coordinate in the coordinate system of the object to be moved.
• The parameter Rotation is a value of data type real which designates a rotation around the negative
z-axis.
You can also specify the parameter Rotation as an array of data type real with four values. It designates
a rotation, compare _3D.Rotation, which the animation point of the object to be moved is to take within
its coordinate system after finishing the pose movement.
• The optional parameter TimeSpan of data type time sets the duration of the movement.
If you do not specify the parameter, Plant Simulation runs the movement run with the time which
results from the speed from the joint definition of this animatable object.
Return Value
The return value has the data type time.
It designates the time span of the simulation time which the designated movement will probably take,
provided it will not be interrupted.
Examples
Station._3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate([1m, 2m, 0m], 0)
Station._3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate([1m, 2m, 0m], 45, 1:00)
Station._3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate([1m, 2m, 0m], [90, 0, 1, 0]) 
Station._3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate(Station._3D.getPositionFromObject(OtherSt
ations), 0)
var Rotation = Station._3D.getRotationFromObject(OtherObject)
Rotation := 
F3DconcatenateRotations(Station._3D.getRotationFromObject(OtherObject), 
[180, 0, 1, 0])
Station._3D.Poses.moveToCoordinate(Station._3D.getPositionFromObject(OtherOb
ject), Rotation) 
// grip 'OtherObject' from above
