_3D.Poses.moveToMU [SimTalk]
Moves the object designated by <Path> with its first animation point to the specified MU instance as long
as the identified robot configuration supports this. This works without you having to define a pose.
Remarks
_3D.Poses.moveToMU does not support the four axis robot with ball joint gripper. The object
corresponds to one of the supported robot configurations.
Plant Simulation checks the attribute _3D.AnimationObject to identify the robot configuration. The
objects, which are located between the simulation object, which is excluded, and _3D.AnimationObject,
which is included, are checked for the appropriate robot configuration with which the pose movement is
executed.
Plant Simulation checks if a component of the axes configuration matches a supported robot type from the
simulation object level to the gripper object. A robot type with more axes takes precedence over a robot
type with fewer axes.
Note
If Plant Simulation cannot identify the robot configuration, this is considered an error and the Method
Debugger will open.
Note
Not reaching the destination MU instance is not considered an error and does not open the Method
Debugger. An unsuitable, but recognizable axes configuration will not be recognized, if eight axes exist,
for example, but only the last three axes are recognized as part of a simpler robot type.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Path>_3D.Poses.moveToMU(MU:object[, TimeSpan:time]) -> time
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter MU of data type object designates the MU instance to which the object is to move.
• The optional parameter TimeSpan of data type time sets the duration of the movement.
_3D.Poses.moveToMU [SimTalk]
12-938 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

If you do not specify the parameter, Plant Simulation executes the movement run with the time which
results from the speed from the joint definition of the involved animatable objects.
Return Value
The return value has the data type time.
It designates the time span of the simulation time which the designated movement will probably take,
provided it will not be interrupted.
Examples
var mu = otherObject.cont
var arm = self.~._3D.getObject("Arm")
wait arm.moveToMU(mu)
-- control MyRob action to pick parts at the store
-- and drop them on the station
var nextPart : object
for var i := 1 to 12
    nextPart = Store.cont
    waituntil station.empty
    -- move to part and load it
    wait MyRob._3D.Poses.MoveToMU(nextPart)
    nextPart.move(MyRob)
    wait 1
    -- move to target station and drop part there
    wait MyRob._3D.Poses.MoveToMUAnimationPosition(nextPart, Station)
    nextPart.move(Station)
    wait 1
    -- move robot to home position
    wait MyRob._3D.Poses.moveTo("home")
next
