_3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
Moves the object designated by <Path> with its first animation point to the position and rotation in space
where the specified MU instance would be located when being moved to the specified place as long as the
identified robot configuration supports this. This works without you having to define a pose.
Remarks
_3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition does not support the four axis robot with ball joint gripper. The
object corresponds to one of the supported robot configurations.
Plant Simulation checks the attribute _3D.AnimationObject to identify the robot configuration. The
objects, which are located between the simulation object, which is excluded, and _3D.AnimationObject,
which is included, are checked for the appropriate robot configuration with which the pose movement is
executed.
Plant Simulation checks if a part of the axes configuration matches a supported robot type from the
simulation object level to the gripper object. A robot type with more axes takes precedence over a robot
type with fewer axes.
If the destination is length-oriented _3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition of animatable objects
computes a movement, which deposits the front of the MU at the destination instead of the booking
point. For place-oriented objects nothing changes.
Note
If Plant Simulation cannot identify the robot configuration, this is considered an error and the Method
Debugger will open.
Note
Not reaching the destination place is not considered an error and does not open the Method Debugger.
An unsuitable, but recognizable axes configuration will not be recognized, if eight axes exist, for
example, but only the last three axes are recognized as part of a simpler robot type.
Note
Plant Simulation does not use joint limits in this method. This means, that you can move to a position
beyond the limits you specified.
Type
Method
_3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 12-941
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Syntax
<Path>_3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition(MU:object, TargetPE:object/PE[, 
RelPos/AbsPos:real/length, TimeSpan:time]) -> time
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter MU of data type object designates the MU instance to be measured when moving to the
target place.
• The parameter TargetPE of data type object designates the place to which the item is to move. This can
be a specific PE (production element), or an object if the object only has one PE.
• The optional parameter RelPos/AbsPos designates a value of data type real or length.
If the value has the data type real, it designates the relative position on the object. The relative position
can be a value between 0 and 1. 0 designates the start of the animation and 1 the end of the animation,
i.e., the value 100 %.
If the value has the data type length, it designates the absolute position on the object as the distance
starting at the beginning of the object.
This parameter will only be used if TargetPE specifies a length-oriented place, e.g. a Conveyor or a
Transporter with a length-oriented load bay.
If you do not specify the parameter, it takes the value 0.0.
• The optional parameter TimeSpan of data type time designates the duration of the movement.
If you do not specify the parameter, the movement is run with the time which results from the speeds
from the joint definitions of the involved animatable objects.
Return Value
The return value has the data type time.
It designates the time span of the simulation time which the designated movement will probably take,
provided it will not be interrupted.
Examples
var mu = otherObject.cont
wait self.~._3D.moveToMUAnimationPosition(mu)
-- control MyRob action to pick parts at the store
-- and drop them on the station
var nextPart : object
_3D.Poses.moveToMUAnimationPosition [SimTalk]
